          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582783,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Early Career and Fauvism</strong><br>\nHenri Matisse was born in 1869 in Le Cateau-Cambrésis, France. Initially studying law, Matisse discovered his passion for art in his early twenties. He became a leader of Fauvism, characterized by its use of bold, undisguised brushstrokes and vibrant colors.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Development of Personal Style</strong><br>\nOver time, Matisse developed his distinctive style, often featuring simplified forms and a stark, expressive use of color. He focused on a range of subjects, from nudes and figures in landscapes to interiors and still lifes, always exploring the expressive potential of color.</p>\n\n<p><strong>\"The Joy of Life\"</strong><br>\nOne of Matisse's most influential works is \"The Joy of Life\" (Le Bonheur de Vivre), a large-scale painting that epitomizes the tenets of Fauvism with its vivid colors, rhythmic lines, and optimistic depiction of a pastoral, Edenic scene.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "53bc5361-5ce5-43a9-a00f-57bbceee9cd0"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          }
        ],
        "slideBackgroundSelector": {}
      },
      {
        "elements": [
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
